{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires implementing `grammar_guided_node_reduction` via `solve(case)`. Let me understand what the algorithm needs to do:\n\n1. We have a parse tree with nodes, each having: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n2. We have required_symbols that must always remain represented in the tree\n3. We need to reduce the tree by removing nodes while:\n   - Never removing root\n   - Never removing a non-removable node\n   - Never removing a node that has retained children\n   - Always keeping at least one node with each required symbol\n4. We consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (bottom-up)\n5. Tie-break by node ID (lexicographic? or as string?)\n6. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented after removal\n7. Return retained_node_ids (sorted) and removed_node_ids (in actual removal order)\n\nLet me think about the algorithm:\n\n1. Build the tree structure\n2. Process nodes in order: deeper first, then by node ID as tiebreak\n3. For each candidate node (removable, no retained children):\n   - Check if removing it would leave all required symbols still represented\n   - If yes, remove it (mark as removed)\n4. Return sorted retained IDs and removal order\n\nLet me trace through case 000:\n- Nodes: root(s=root, d=0, rem=false), n1(s=s1, d=1, rem=false), n2(s=s2, d=1, rem=true), n3(s=s0, d=1, rem=false), n4(s=s1, d=2, rem=true), n5(s=s2, d=2, rem=false), n6(s=s0, d=2, rem=true)\n- required_symbols: root, s1\n\nProcessing order (deeper first, then by ID):\n- depth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by ID: n4, n5, n6)\n- depth 1: n1, n2, n3 (sorted by ID: n1, n2, n3)\n- depth 0: root\n\nFor n4 (s=s1, d=2, rem=true):\n- Currently retained symbols with s1: n1(s1), n4(s1)\n- If we remove n4, s1 is still represented by n1\n- n4 has no children (retained)\n- Can we remove n4? Yes, required symbols still covered\n- Remove n4. removed=[n4]\n\nFor n5 (s=s2, d=2, rem=false):\n- Not removable, skip\n\nFor n6 (s=s0, d=2, rem=true):\n- Currently retained symbols: root has root, s1 has n1, s0 has n3 and n6\n- If we remove n6, s0 still represented by n3\n- But wait, is s0 in required_symbols? No, required_symbols = [root, s1]\n- n6 has no retained children\n- Can we remove n6? Required symbols are root and s1. After removing n6: root still has root(id=root), s1 still has n1. Yes.\n- Remove n6. removed=[n4, n6]\n\nNow depth 1:\nFor n1 (s=s1, d=1, rem=false):\n- Not removable, skip\n\nFor n2 (s=s2, d=1, rem=true):\n- Check retained children: n4 was removed, so n2 has no retained children\n- If we remove n2, s2 is... s2 not in required_symbols, so OK\n- Remove n2. removed=[n4, n6, n2]\n\nFor n3 (s=s0, d=1, rem=false):\n- Not removable, skip\n\nFor root (d=0, rem=false):\n- Not removable, skip\n\nSo retained: root, n1, n3, n5\nSorted: n1, n3, n5, root\nRemoved in order: n4, n6, n2\n\nWait, but \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - what does \"sorted\" mean? Alphabetically? By some other criterion?\n\nLooking at the output schema, retained_node_ids is just an array of strings. \"Sorted\" likely means sorted lexicographically/alphabetically.\n\nLet me sort: n1, n3, n5, root → alphabetically: n1, n3, n5, root\n\nActually let me re-check. IDs are: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6. Alphabetically: n1 \u003c n2 \u003c n3 \u003c n4 \u003c n5 \u003c n6 \u003c root.\n\nSo retained sorted: [n1, n3, n5, root]\n\nNow let me also reconsider the processing order. \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\nSo we sort by (depth DESC, node_id ASC). Let me re-verify:\n\ndepth=2: n4, n5, n6 (in order: n4, n5, n6 alphabetically)\ndepth=1: n1, n2, n3 (in order: n1, n2, n3 alphabetically)\ndepth=0: root\n\nThis matches what I did above.\n\nNow let me also check: \"Never remove a node with retained children\" - this means we need to dynamically check if a node has any currently retained children before attempting removal.\n\nLet me re-trace more carefully:\n\nInitial state: all nodes retained.\nchildren map:\n- root: [n1, n2, n3]\n- n1: [n6]\n- n2: [n4]\n- n3: [n5]\n- n4: []\n- n5: []\n- n6: []\n\nProcessing order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root\n\nn4: removable=true, retained_children=[] (empty), \n  - Symbols after removal: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2), n6(s0) → root✓, s1✓\n  - Remove n4. removed=[n4]\n\nn5: removable=false → skip\n\nn6: removable=true, retained_children=[] (empty)\n  - Symbols after removal: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2) → root✓, s1✓\n  - Remove n6. removed=[n4, n6]\n\nn1: removable=false → skip\n\nn2: removable=true, retained_children of n2 = [n4] but n4 is removed, so retained_children=[]\n  - Symbols after removal: root(root), n1(s1), n3(s0), n5(s2) → root✓, s1✓\n  - Remove n2. removed=[n4, n6, n2]\n\nn3: removable=false → skip\n\nroot: removable=false → skip\n\nRetained: root, n1, n3, n5\nSorted: [n1, n3, n5, root]\nRemoved: [n4, n6, n2]\n\nNow let me implement this in Python:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build lookup structures\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retention status\n    retained = {n[\"id\"]: True for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by ID ascending\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if has retained children\n        if any(retained[c] for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Check if all required symbols remain after removal\n        # Count symbols among retained nodes excluding this node\n        symbol_counts = {}\n        for n in nodes:\n            if retained[n[\"id\"]] and n[\"id\"] != nid:\n                sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n                symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        \n        if all(sym in symbol_counts for sym in required_symbols):\n            retained[nid] = False\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid, r in retained.items() if r)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nWait, the symbol_counts check is a bit inefficient but works for small trees.\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n- What if a required symbol only has one node? Then we can't remove that node.\n- The \"symbol_counts\" approach correctly handles this.\n\nLet me trace through case 001 to verify:\n\nNodes: root(root,d=0,rem=false), n1(s1,d=1,rem=false), n2(s2,d=1,rem=true), n3(s0,d=1,rem=false), n4(s1,d=2,rem=true), n5(s2,d=2,rem=false), n6(s0,d=2,rem=true)\nrequired_symbols: root, s1\n\nProcessing order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root\n\nn4 (s1, removable=true):\n  - retained_children=[]\n  - After removing n4, s1 still covered by n1\n  - Remove. removed=[n4]\n\nn5 (s2, removable=false): skip\n\nn6 (s0, removable=true):\n  - retained_children=[]\n  - After removing n6: root✓, s1 covered by n1 ✓\n  - Remove. removed=[n4, n6]\n\nn1 (s1, removable=false): skip\n\nn2 (s2, removable=true):\n  - retained_children=[n4] but n4 removed → no retained children\n  - After removing n2: root✓, s1 covered by n1 ✓\n  - Remove. removed=[n4, n6, n2]\n\nn3 (s0, removable=false): skip\n\nroot: not removable, skip\n\nRetained: root, n1, n3, n5\nSorted retained: [n1, n3, n5, root]\nRemoved: [n4, n6, n2]\n\nSame result as case 000. Makes sense since the structure is the same, just different utility values (utility values don't seem to be used in the algorithm directly based on the description).\n\nWait, I should re-read: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The utility might be relevant to interestingness. But the spec says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this is the interestingness check. The utility doesn't seem to play a direct role in the removal decision.\n\nActually, let me re-read more carefully. \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" - Perses is a tree-based program reducer that removes subtrees. The key property is: after removal, all required symbols must still be covered. This matches what I implemented.\n\nBut wait - \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\" - this is about ordering candidates. The ordering is: deeper first, then by node ID ascending. Let me double-check this interpretation is correct.\n\nLooking at the candidate_artifact description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" This means we process in order: (-depth, node_id), which is what I have.\n\nLet me also verify case 002:\n\nSame structure, same required_symbols. The algorithm doesn't depend on utility values, so the result should be the same: retained=[n1, n3, n5, root], removed=[n4, n6, n2].\n\nNow the implementation looks correct. Let me also add the validation as mentioned: \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\"\n\nWhat validations should we do?\n1. Required fields present (nodes, required_symbols)\n2. Each node has required fields (id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility)\n3. Parent references are valid (or null for root)\n4. At least one node for each required symbol exists initially\n\nLet me write the final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate structure\n    for n in nodes:\n        for field in (\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"):\n            if field not in n:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing field: {field}\")\n    \n    node_ids = {n[\"id\"] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None and n[\"parent\"] not in node_ids:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unknown parent: {n['parent']}\")\n    \n    # Validate required symbols are covered initially\n    symbols_present = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes}\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbols_present:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol not present: {sym}\")\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retention status\n    retained = {n[\"id\"]: True for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by ID ascending (stable tie-break)\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if has any retained children\n        if any(retained[c] for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Check if all required symbols remain after tentative removal\n        symbol_counts = {}\n        for n in nodes:\n            if retained[n[\"id\"]] and n[\"id\"] != nid:\n                sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n                symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        \n        if all(sym in symbol_counts for sym in required_symbols):\n            retained[nid] = False\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid, r in retained.items() if r)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks good. Now I need to format it as the required JSON response with the \"implementation\" key, with the Python code as a JSON string.\n\nThe output must be:\n```json\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n```\n\nLet me format the Python code properly as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet me write it out carefully:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        for field in (\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"):\n            if field not in n:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing field: {field}\")\n    \n    node_ids = {n[\"id\"] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None and n[\"parent\"] not in node_ids:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unknown parent: {n['parent']}\")\n    \n    symbols_present = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes}\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbols_present:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol not present: {sym}\")\n    \n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    retained = {n[\"id\"]: True for n in nodes}\n    \n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        if any(retained[c] for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        symbol_counts = {}\n        for n in nodes:\n            if retained[n[\"id\"]] and n[\"id\"] != nid:\n                sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n                symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        \n        if all(sym in symbol_counts for sym in required_symbols):\n            retained[nid] = False\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid, r in retained.items() if r)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nNow I need to JSON-encode this. Let me be careful about the f-strings with single quotes inside - those are fine in Python but need to make sure they're properly escaped in JSON.\n\nThe f-string `f\"Unknown parent: {n['parent']}\"` contains single quotes, which don't need escaping in JSON strings.\n\nLet me construct the JSON carefully. I'll write the implementation as a single JSON string value.\n\nThe response must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\".\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Validate required fields on every node\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        for field in (\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"):\\n            if field not in n:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing required field: {field}\\\")\\n\\n    # Validate parent references\\n    node_ids = {n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None and n[\\\"parent\\\"] not in node_ids:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Unknown parent id: {n['parent']}\\\")\\n\\n    # Validate required symbols are initially present\\n    symbols_present = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbols_present:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol not present in tree: {sym}\\\")\\n\\n    # Build children map (parent -\u003e list of child ids)\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    # Track which nodes are currently retained\\n    retained = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: True for n in nodes}\\n\\n    # Process candidates: deeper nodes first, tie-break by node id ascending\\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Skip non-removable nodes\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Skip nodes that still have retained children\\n        if any(retained[c] for c in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Tentatively remove: check all required symbols remain represented\\n        symbol_counts = {}\\n        for n in nodes:\\n            if retained[n[\\\"id\\\"]] and n[\\\"id\\\"] != nid:\\n                sym = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n                symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n\\n        if all(sym in symbol_counts for sym in required_symbols):\\n            retained[nid] = False\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid, r in retained.items() if r)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01LARa52LNmJGqqRQWbJcnvM","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2561},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2561,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":31,"output_tokens":4967,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4295},"service_tier":"standard"}}